/* book Worked example 2: Q: A bridged-T attenuator is the T network above (Z₁ = 2 Ω, Z₂ = 3 Ω, Z₃ = 4 Ω) with a 5 Ω resistor joined directly from terminal 1 to terminal 2. Find its Y parameters as a parallel connection and its input resistance with port 2 open.
   1. Both parts are three-terminal networks sharing the common terminal, so the parallel rule is valid.
   2. T network: [Ya] = [Za]⁻¹ = (1/26)[7 −4; −4 6] = [0.2692 −0.1538; −0.1538 0.2308] S.
   3. 5 Ω bridge (a series element): [Yb] = [0.2 −0.2; −0.2 0.2] S.
   4. [Y] = [0.4692 −0.3538; −0.3538 0.4308] S (exactly 61/130, −46/130 and 56/130 S).
   5. Δy = 0.4692 × 0.4308 − 0.3538² = 0.2021 − 0.1252 = 0.0769 S² (exactly 1/13). With port 2 open, Rin = z₁₁ = y₂₂/Δy = 0.4308 × 13 = 5.6 Ω.
   6. Check by reduction: with port 2 open, the 5 Ω bridge and the 3 Ω arm form an 8 Ω path from terminal 1 to the centre node, in parallel with the 2 Ω arm: 2 × 8/10 = 1.6 Ω. Add the 4 Ω shunt arm: 5.6 Ω ✓.
   Answer: [Y] = [0.4692 −0.3538; −0.3538 0.4308] S, and the input resistance with port 2 open is 5.6 Ω. */
